Id: no-channel-type-outside-surface
Pattern: \b(IChannel|ChannelItemInfo|ChannelItemResult|ChannelItemType|ChannelFolderType|ChannelMediaContentType|ChannelMediaType|ChannelItemSortField|InternalChannelItemQuery|InternalChannelFeatures|ChannelParentalRating|DynamicImageResponse|MetadataProvider|ImageType|ImageFormat|BaseItem|MediaSourceInfo|PersonInfo)\b|MediaBrowser\.(Controller\.(Channels|Entities|Providers|Library|LiveTv|Dto)|Model\.(Channels|Entities|Drawing|Dto|LiveTv))\b
Subject: *.cs
Except: :!*SurfaceAdapter.cs :!*SurfaceAdapterTests.cs :!*PluginServiceRegistrator.cs
Issue: 52

A discover page is a server channel, and the server's channel types are the
shape that decision produced rather than the shape this plugin thinks in. If
one of them reaches the shelf code, the catalogue code or the seam, then
changing how the surface is represented means changing everything that touched
it, and the adapter that exists to absorb that change absorbs nothing.

The pattern has two halves and they cover each other. The names are the types
the adapter actually reaches for, which catches a file naming one with no
import in sight. The namespaces catch everything else in the same places: a
type from them is either imported or written out in full, and both spellings
carry the namespace, so the second half does not depend on anybody having
listed the type. Neither half alone would do. A file can name `ChannelItemInfo`
with a global import that this tree does not have, and a file can reach a type
nobody thought to list.

Crossing the boundary is not only a matter of the channel vocabulary, which is
why the entity, provider and drawing namespaces are in the second half. The
item base class, the media source, the person and the picture kinds all arrive
through the same interface, and an item shape that leaks is as expensive as a
folder kind that does. `MediaBrowser.Model.Plugins`, `MediaBrowser.Common` and
`MediaBrowser.Model.Serialization` are deliberately not here: the plugin class
and its configuration are how a plugin is loaded at all, and they are not the
surface.

The exception is the surface adapter itself, which is the one place the
server's vocabulary is allowed to be spoken, and its own tests, which have to
speak it to call the adapter at all. Every other test goes through the plugin's
own interface and a fake of it, which is what #49 asks for; a test file that has
to be renamed to compile is the rule telling its author that it is on the wrong
side of the boundary.

The registrator is the third, added with the registration in #53, and it is the
same case rather than a hole cut for convenience. The server builds its surfaces
by asking its container for every implementation of `IChannel`, so a plugin that
registers under any other type is one the server never collects and no client
ever sees. That name cannot be avoided in the place a plugin declares what it
offers, and the declaration is what registration is. It is the same reasoning
this rule already gives for the adapter: if the surface ever stops being
represented as a channel, both files change, because both are the seam to the
server rather than code behind it. What is on the other side of that seam, the
shelves, the catalogue and the handover, still names none of it.

The file is one line of the vocabulary wide today, and nothing here holds it to
one line. What holds the registrator narrow is the test that exactly one surface
is offered to the server, which derives the interface from the adapter rather
than naming it, so it stays silent under this rule while counting what this
exception makes possible.
